.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt GETPRIORITY 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm getpriority ,
.Nm setpriority ,
.Nm nice
.Nd get or set the nice value of threads
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/resource.h
.Ft int
.Fn getpriority "int which" "id_t who"
.Ft int
.Fn setpriority "int which" "id_t who" "int prio"
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn nice "int inc"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each thread has a nice value from -20 to 19, 0 at boot, as on Linux.
.Xr fork 2
and
.Xr clone 2
give the new thread the caller's, and
.Xr execve 2
keeps it.
.Pp
.Fa which
and
.Fa who
select the threads:
.Bl -tag -width "PRIO_PROCESS"
.It Dv PRIO_PROCESS
The thread whose ID is
.Fa who ;
a process's ID is its first thread's, and selects that thread alone.
.It Dv PRIO_PGRP
Every thread of the processes in the process group
.Fa who .
.It Dv PRIO_USER
Every thread whose real user ID is
.Fa who .
.El
.Pp
A
.Fa who
of 0 selects the calling thread, its process group, or its real user ID.
A zombie not yet reaped is selected like any thread, as on Linux.
.Pp
.Fn getpriority
returns the lowest nice value among the selected threads.
.Pp
.Fn setpriority
sets the nice value of each selected thread to
.Fa prio ,
clamped to the range -20 through 19.
A caller with effective user ID 0 may change any thread.
Any other caller may change a thread only when its effective user ID
equals that thread's real or effective user ID, and may lower the nice
value only as far as the thread's
.Dv RLIMIT_NICE
allows, 20 less its soft value, as on Linux; at the default of 0 it may
only raise it.
When some selected threads may not be changed, the others are changed
and the call fails with the error of the last refusal.
.Pp
.Fn nice
adds
.Fa inc
to the caller's nice value, with the same range for the result.
.Pp
As on Linux, a lower nice value gives an ordinary thread a larger
weight, and hence a larger share of a processor when runnable threads
compete in the same scheduling group.
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
uses a fixed weight of 3 for any nice value.
The real-time policies use their real-time priorities.
Changing a thread's nice value leaves its autogroup's nice value alone.
See
.Xr sched 7 .
.Pa /proc/PID/stat
reports the first thread's nice value as field 19.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 96 for
.Fn getpriority
and 97 for
.Fn setpriority .
EBX contains
.Fa which ,
ECX
.Fa who ,
and EDX the nice value.
On x86-64 the numbers are 140 and 141, with the same arguments in RDI, RSI
and RDX.
.Pp
The x86-64 kernel also runs i386's
.Nm nice
(34) for a program that enters it by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
as
.Xr ia32 7
describes: it adds the increment in EBX, held to -40 through 40, to the
caller's nice value, which stays within -20 to 19, and returns 0.
A negative increment to a value
.Dv RLIMIT_NICE
does not allow fails without privilege with
.Er EPERM ,
as on Linux, whose default of 0 allows none.
The i686 kernel has no
.Nm nice
system call, which Linux has on i386.
On both of Rach2's architectures,
musl implements
.Fn nice
through
.Fn setpriority .
As on Linux, the raw
.Fn getpriority
returns 20 less the nice value, 40 for -20 through 1 for 19, so that no
success is negative; the C library returns the nice value itself.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn getpriority
returns the nice value, which may be -1, so a caller that needs to tell
an error from a nice value of -1 sets
.Va errno
to 0 first and reads it afterwards.
.Fn setpriority
returns 0.
.Fn nice
returns the new nice value through the C library; set
.Va errno
to 0 first to tell a successful return of -1 from an error.
On failure these functions return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/schedmusl.c
sets and reads the nice value of the process, its group and a user,
checks that a child inherits it, that the raw call returns 20 less it,
and that a caller of another user ID is refused both another user's
process and a lower nice value of its own.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EACCES]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
A caller without effective user ID 0 asked to lower a nice value past
what the thread's
.Dv RLIMIT_NICE
allows.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa which
is none of the three.
.It Bq Er EPERM
A caller without effective user ID 0 selected a thread whose real and
effective user IDs both differ from its effective user ID.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No thread was selected.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr ioprio_set 2 ,
.Xr sched_setscheduler 2 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr sched 7
